Abstract = {We propose a method of assessing party influence, based on a spatial
   model, Our method provides the first test of whether observed values of
   the widely-used Rice index of party dissimilarity are consistent with a
   ``partyless{''} null model. It also avoids problems that beset previous
   estimators. Substantively, we find evidence of influence in all but one
   Congress since 1877. Moreover, our indicator of party pressure is
   systematically higher for the sorts of roll calls that party theorists
   believe are more pressured-procedural, organizational, and
   label-defining votes. Our results refute the widespread notion that
   parties in the House have typically had negligible influence on
   roll-call voting behavior. They also document important changes In party
   influence associated with the packing of the Rules Committee in 1961 and
   the procedural reforms of 1973.},
